  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-129628",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, we factor the numerator and denominator of $f(x)$:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{x+1}{2{x}^{2}-11x+5}=\\frac{x+1}{(2x-1)(x-5)}\n$$\n\nOur task is to find $A$ and $B$ such that\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+1}{(2x-1)(x-5)}=\\frac{A}{2x-1}+\\frac{B}{x-5}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $(2x-1)(x-5)$ gives\n\n$$\nx+1=A(x-5)+B(2x-1).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5+1 & =A(5-5)+B(2\\cdot 5-1) \\\\ 6 & =9B \\\\ B & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{1}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}+1 & =A(\\frac{1}{2}-5)+B(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & =-\\frac{9}{2}A \\\\ A & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+1}{(2x-1)(x-5)}=\\frac{2}{3(x-5)}-\\frac{1}{3(2x-1)}.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }(\\frac{2}{3(x-5)}-\\frac{1}{3(2x-1)})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x-5}-\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{2x-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $A=-\\frac{1}{3}$ and $B=\\frac{2}{3}.$",
